8250: CE5300 GEN3 UARTs are XScale — force the type so UUE is set

The three SoC UARTs on the Intel CE5300 (EA family) are an XScale/PXA block --
Control4's kernel renames PORT_XSCALE's uart_config to "GEN3_serial" -- and the
IO microcontroller (relays, contacts, IR, combo-serial) hangs off ttyS1 at legacy
I/O 0x2F8. An XScale port only works with UART_CAP_UUE: the UART Unit Enable bit
(IER b6) that gates whether the block drives its pins at all. Without it the port
transmits into a dead pad and never receives -- exactly what the IO-MCU did (tx
counted, rx=0 forever, so gpio-ohc-iomcu never registered a gpiochip/lirc).

autoconfig_16550a already knows how to find an XScale part -- it toggles IER b6
and, if the bit sticks, sets PORT_XSCALE + UART_CAP_UUE|UART_CAP_RTOIE. On a stock
CE5300 (3.12) that probe succeeds and ttyS0/1/3 come up as GEN3_serial. On this
kernel it does NOT: when autoconfig runs the block is not clocked yet, the UUE bit
does not latch, and all three fall through to plain 16550A. (The same bit is
writable once the port is opened -- measured on hardware -- so it is a probe timing
problem, not missing silicon.)

Rather than fight the probe timing, force the type for the three fixed SoC I/O
bases (0x3F8 console, 0x2F8 IO-MCU, 0x2E8) and leave the UUE bit enabled. This
keeps them as normal I/O-mapped ISA ports -- declaring PORT_XSCALE + UPF_FIXED_TYPE
in SERIAL_PORT_DFNS instead makes serial8250_isa_init_ports drop them and the 2e66
PCI UARTs steal the ttyS numbers. The 8051 power PIC at 0x3E8 is a genuine 8250 and
is deliberately not matched, matching stock's /proc/tty/driver/serial. Pairs with
patch 0002 (clock + IRQ 38).

Scope: EA family only (board/ea-common/patches).

Author: Joseph Telaak <joseph@telaak.dev>
---
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_port.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_port.c
@@ -1021,6 +1021,25 @@
 	}
 
 	/*
+	 * Intel CE5300 (EA family): three of the legacy I/O UARTs are the SoC's
+	 * own XScale/PXA block -- "GEN3_serial" in Control4's kernel -- with the
+	 * IO microcontroller on ttyS1 (0x2F8). They MUST run with UART_CAP_UUE
+	 * (the Unit Enable bit) or the block never drives its pins, plus
+	 * UART_CAP_RTOIE so a short reply's rx-timeout interrupt fires. The generic
+	 * UUE probe just below is unreliable here: at early autoconfig the unit is
+	 * not clocked yet, the bit does not latch, and the port falls through to a
+	 * plain 16550A -- a deaf IO-MCU. Force the type by the fixed SoC I/O bases;
+	 * the 8051 PIC at 0x3E8 is a genuine 8250 and is deliberately not matched.
+	 */
+	if (up->port.iobase == 0x3F8 || up->port.iobase == 0x2F8 ||
+	    up->port.iobase == 0x2E8) {
+		up->port.type = PORT_XSCALE;
+		up->capabilities |= UART_CAP_UUE | UART_CAP_RTOIE;
+		serial_out(up, UART_IER, UART_IER_UUE);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * Try writing and reading the UART_IER_UUE bit (b6).
 	 * If it works, this is probably one of the Xscale platform's
 	 * internal UARTs.
